WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:11.190 --> 00:00:13.848
The satisfying part in
repairing and restoring a watch

00:00:13.848 --> 00:00:16.006
is when you're bringing it back to life.

00:00:17.370 --> 00:00:18.450
My name is Roland.

00:00:18.450 --> 00:00:20.640
I'm the founder of RGM Watch Co.,

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:24.690
and we've been restoring
watches for almost 40 years.

00:00:24.690 --> 00:00:26.340
We're going to be restoring today

00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:30.360
a vintage Rolex military pocket watch

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:33.150
from the World War II era.

00:00:33.150 --> 00:00:34.470
The first thing that we'll do

00:00:34.470 --> 00:00:38.580
is we will visually
examine it as one piece.

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:42.390
All the parts are original parts on it.

00:00:42.390 --> 00:00:44.880
Certain things will be
refinished and improved;

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:46.950
other things will be left
the way that they are

00:00:46.950 --> 00:00:49.020
to preserve the original.

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:50.910
But when it comes to the
mechanics of the watch,

00:00:50.910 --> 00:00:52.740
of course that's all vital.

00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:55.770
In the end, we wanted this
watch to work very well,

00:00:55.770 --> 00:00:58.833
but we wanted it to look good, too.

00:00:58.833 --> 00:01:00.450
When disassembling the watch,

00:01:00.450 --> 00:01:01.999
generally the first thing we'll do

00:01:01.999 --> 00:01:05.760
is remove the case back
and look at the movement.

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:08.242
The movement is the
mechanism inside the watch.

00:01:08.242 --> 00:01:11.130
It would almost be like
saying the engine in a car.

00:01:11.130 --> 00:01:13.155
I can see, visually,

00:01:13.155 --> 00:01:16.770
if there's anything that
jumps out to me that's broken.

00:01:16.770 --> 00:01:19.350
I'm going to remove the balance bridge

00:01:19.350 --> 00:01:20.880
and the balance wheel.

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:23.577
The balance wheel is one
of the most fragile parts,

00:01:23.577 --> 00:01:26.010
so we want to get that
out, off to the side.

00:01:26.010 --> 00:01:29.640
Next, we will remove the winding wheels.

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:32.880
At that point, we can generally
remove the barrel bridge,

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:36.300
which is over top of
the mainspring barrel.

00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:41.010
Then we would move on to
removing the train wheel bridge.

00:01:41.010 --> 00:01:42.730
Then we're kind of
getting it down to where

00:01:42.730 --> 00:01:45.630
we're going to have wheels by themselves.

00:01:45.630 --> 00:01:48.982
Of course, that escape
wheel will come out.

00:01:48.982 --> 00:01:52.530
And then we basically have it
torn down to the basic parts.

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:56.730
Now that we have the watch disassembled,

00:01:56.730 --> 00:02:00.930
the next step is to start
the cleaning of the parts.

00:02:00.930 --> 00:02:04.492
We have different types
of cleaning baskets

00:02:04.492 --> 00:02:07.740
and trays that we can put those parts in.

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:10.290
We have little teeny
cylinders that screw together

00:02:10.290 --> 00:02:12.240
that can hold tiny parts.

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:15.000
The main cleaning basket itself has trays,

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:18.030
so that's very useful in
keeping the parts organized

00:02:18.030 --> 00:02:20.670
and also preventing them from being lost.

00:02:20.670 --> 00:02:24.900
We will generally start
in a tank ultrasonic.

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:28.849
It's just helping to really
ultrasonically loosen

00:02:28.849 --> 00:02:31.080
the dirt particles.

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:35.250
We will then manually clean parts also.

00:02:35.250 --> 00:02:39.150
With pocket watches, we often
have to scrape the jewels,

00:02:39.150 --> 00:02:42.180
do a lot of manual cleaning,

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:45.150
a lot of scraping and
cleaning, pegging the holes,

00:02:45.150 --> 00:02:47.520
and things like along those lines.

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:50.490
We have a glass jar with hexane in it,

00:02:50.490 --> 00:02:54.783
and we use that to clean
parts at the bench.

00:02:54.783 --> 00:02:57.930
We just use air to dry the hexane,

00:02:57.930 --> 00:03:01.244
which will dry quite quickly.

00:03:01.244 --> 00:03:02.630
And then at the very end it will go

00:03:02.630 --> 00:03:05.490
into a watch-cleaning machine.

00:03:05.490 --> 00:03:07.230
It has a solution in it for cleaning,

00:03:07.230 --> 00:03:09.930
but it also has the physical agitation

00:03:09.930 --> 00:03:12.030
of the basket turning back and forth.

00:03:12.030 --> 00:03:14.442
So the combination of all that

00:03:14.442 --> 00:03:17.310
will really get the parts very clean.

00:03:17.310 --> 00:03:19.800
Then they'll be rinsed the same way,

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:22.320
and then it goes into a drying sequence

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:24.180
where it's also spinning,

00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:27.510
and that prevents spotting
and things like that.

00:03:27.510 --> 00:03:30.660
Now that we have the
preliminary cleaning done,

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:33.450
we can get into actually doing the repair

00:03:33.450 --> 00:03:36.840
and the refinishing of certain parts.

00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:38.023
In repairing this watch,

00:03:38.023 --> 00:03:40.989
a very crucial area is
always the escapement

00:03:40.989 --> 00:03:44.277
and the balance, hairspring.

00:03:44.277 --> 00:03:49.143
That's sort of the heart of the watch.

00:03:49.143 --> 00:03:52.410
This particular watch is not running.

00:03:52.410 --> 00:03:56.280
The work that we're doing
is going to rectify that.

00:03:56.280 --> 00:03:58.603
Here we have the balance wheel

00:03:58.603 --> 00:04:01.140
being put on the poising table.

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:04.650
It will be lightly touched and turned.

00:04:04.650 --> 00:04:07.410
So, when we find a heavy spot,

00:04:07.410 --> 00:04:11.430
we will then remove a little bit of metal.

00:04:11.430 --> 00:04:14.490
This is a very crucial aspect

00:04:14.490 --> 00:04:17.220
of getting good timing out of the watch.

00:04:17.220 --> 00:04:19.020
Sometimes, especially with older watches,

00:04:19.020 --> 00:04:22.230
when we get it, the hairspring
has been maybe mistreated.

00:04:22.230 --> 00:04:26.670
So we need to try to get the
coil spacing nice and even,

00:04:26.670 --> 00:04:28.467
and then we want to
make sure the hairspring

00:04:28.467 --> 00:04:30.420
is flat over the balance wheel

00:04:30.420 --> 00:04:32.763
and not tilted or touching something.

00:04:33.630 --> 00:04:37.500
So there's minute adjustments
we have to do with tweezers

00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:41.105
to get the hairspring back
into the proper shape.

00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:44.220
The only part that was replaced

00:04:44.220 --> 00:04:46.410
in this watch was the mainspring.

00:04:46.410 --> 00:04:48.360
That's where the power comes from.

00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:50.010
So we have to take the mainspring

00:04:50.010 --> 00:04:51.840
and replace it with a new one

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:56.070
so that the watch will have
enough power to run properly.

00:04:56.070 --> 00:04:57.761
So, the new mainspring,

00:04:57.761 --> 00:05:00.752
wound into the mainspring winder,

00:05:00.752 --> 00:05:03.060
is being pushed from that tool

00:05:03.060 --> 00:05:06.360
into the watch's mainspring barrel.

00:05:06.360 --> 00:05:10.110
And then the barrel arbor will go

00:05:10.110 --> 00:05:11.880
in the center of that mainspring,

00:05:11.880 --> 00:05:16.633
and then we have a lid that
will go onto the barrel.

00:05:20.850 --> 00:05:24.570
Some parts, it's more aesthetics.

00:05:24.570 --> 00:05:27.030
The watch hands were
in pretty rough shape,

00:05:27.030 --> 00:05:30.047
so we made the decision to remove

00:05:30.047 --> 00:05:32.340
most of the rust and oxidation.

00:05:32.340 --> 00:05:35.730
That way, we could get
them looking much nicer.

00:05:35.730 --> 00:05:38.610
Sometimes, depending on the watch,

00:05:38.610 --> 00:05:41.400
the engraving would have paint in it,

00:05:41.400 --> 00:05:44.400
so we will redo that, we'll
clean that really good,

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:48.210
and we'll put a black
enamel paint in there

00:05:48.210 --> 00:05:49.955
and then let it dry.

00:05:50.820 --> 00:05:53.070
Then we can read the
name on it nicely again,

00:05:53.070 --> 00:05:55.023
the way it originally was.

00:05:56.283 --> 00:05:59.130
On this watch, the case was a challenge.

00:05:59.130 --> 00:06:03.025
Someone had tried to remove
the bezel and the back

00:06:03.025 --> 00:06:05.580
with a knife at some point in its life

00:06:05.580 --> 00:06:07.740
and damaged the threads.

00:06:07.740 --> 00:06:11.509
So we had to carefully
rework those threads

00:06:11.509 --> 00:06:14.190
so we could screw the back and bezel on.

00:06:14.190 --> 00:06:18.300
The case obviously had damage.

00:06:18.300 --> 00:06:21.510
Some people like their cases
polished on old watches,

00:06:21.510 --> 00:06:23.520
and other people like
to leave them original,

00:06:23.520 --> 00:06:25.560
with that patina on it.

00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:28.491
We'll make an assessment, you know,

00:06:28.491 --> 00:06:32.940
restoration versus preserving
what's already there.

00:06:32.940 --> 00:06:35.370
In some situations, where
it was already polished

00:06:35.370 --> 00:06:36.900
or the case is damaged,

00:06:36.900 --> 00:06:40.276
the best solution may be to polish it.

00:06:41.426 --> 00:06:44.130
Now we're starting the reassembly.

00:06:44.130 --> 00:06:46.320
We're putting in the escape wheel,

00:06:46.320 --> 00:06:48.870
fourth wheel, third wheel.

00:06:48.870 --> 00:06:51.780
We're putting in the mainspring barrel,

00:06:51.780 --> 00:06:56.547
the center wheel, the
bridges that hold them down.

00:06:56.547 --> 00:06:59.283
We'll then put in the winding wheels.

00:07:00.180 --> 00:07:02.940
Certain things will be lubricated.

00:07:02.940 --> 00:07:05.640
There's a number of different
types of lubrication

00:07:05.640 --> 00:07:08.970
we have to use, different
oils and greases.

00:07:08.970 --> 00:07:11.820
Then the balance will be
attached to the balance bridge,

00:07:11.820 --> 00:07:13.350
and then, of course, that'll be dropped

00:07:13.350 --> 00:07:15.600
into the movement also.

00:07:15.600 --> 00:07:17.725
The satisfying part, you know,

00:07:17.725 --> 00:07:19.058
when you got things cleaned

00:07:19.058 --> 00:07:21.202
and you first put in that balance

00:07:21.202 --> 00:07:23.827
and there's those first few ticks

00:07:23.827 --> 00:07:25.818
and it's running and you're looking at it,

00:07:25.818 --> 00:07:27.210
and you may be the first person

00:07:27.210 --> 00:07:30.660
to see this watch running
in maybe a half a century.

00:07:30.660 --> 00:07:33.420
You know, that's a very satisfying moment.

00:07:33.420 --> 00:07:37.503
On the dial side, there are setting parts.

00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:41.703
We also have the minute
wheel and the hour wheel.

00:07:43.890 --> 00:07:45.543
From that point then, basically,

00:07:45.543 --> 00:07:47.733
it will then be put back into the case.

00:07:50.610 --> 00:07:52.860
And then we'll check
how the finished watch

00:07:52.860 --> 00:07:54.360
is actually running.

00:07:55.740 --> 00:07:58.890
This watch certainly is in
far better condition now

00:07:58.890 --> 00:08:01.623
than it was the day we received it.

00:08:03.234 --> 00:08:07.163
I think it represents a
nice vintage piece now.

00:08:08.250 --> 00:08:10.991
It's a pleasing example

00:08:10.991 --> 00:08:14.032
of this early Rolex military watch.

00:08:14.940 --> 00:08:17.871
And it is, it's a beautiful watch.

